'use strict';
// Original complementary foundation teaching; no human approval is implied.
const C=require('../index'), questions=require('./foundations-questions');
const SID='programming-foundations', lessons=[];
const COMMIT='42155a0feaca9ba6991942c323cbe9fb7278be3c';
const refs={interpreter:'Doc/tutorial/interpreter.rst',introduction:'Doc/tutorial/introduction.rst',execution:'Doc/reference/executionmodel.rst',memory:'Doc/c-api/memory.rst',numbers:'Doc/tutorial/floatingpoint.rst',control:'Doc/tutorial/controlflow.rst',collections:'Doc/tutorial/datastructures.rst',functions:'Doc/tutorial/controlflow.rst'};
function add(id,comp,title,introduction,sections,worked,mistakes,takeaways,references,levels){
 const objective=C.competency(SID+'.'+comp);
 lessons.push({id,module:objective.module,version:1,title,minutes:35,objectives:[{id:SID+'.'+comp,text:objective.competency.text}],prerequisites:lessons.length?[lessons[lessons.length-1].id]:[],prerequisite_note:lessons.length?'Use the previous lesson’s vocabulary and trace notation. Earlier lessons remain available for reference.':'Start here without programming experience. You can follow every example on paper; running code is optional.',introduction,sections,worked,mistakes,takeaways,references,reuse:levels.map(level=>({track:'cs104-python',level,relationship:'supplementary_practice'})),quiz:questions[id].slice(0,3).map(q=>q.id)});
}
add('pf-source-execution','m1.c1','From a written instruction to a running program',
 'A source file is a written description of instructions, not a little processor hidden inside a document. Something must read that description and turn it into operations the machine can perform. This lesson builds a useful model of compilation and interpretation so you can locate a failure before changing random lines of a program.',
 [
  {heading:'Separate source text, translation and execution',paragraphs:[
   'Source code is text written in a programming language. A language defines permitted notation and its meaning. An implementation supplies tools that process that notation. The CPU ultimately executes machine instructions. A compiler translates a representation into another representation, often machine code or an intermediate form. An interpreter performs the behavior of instructions through an executing implementation. Real systems can combine these approaches rather than belonging to two exclusive categories.',
   'For example, a conventional C toolchain compiles and links a program before its executable is run. CPython, a common Python implementation, normally compiles source into bytecode and executes that bytecode using its interpreter. Bytecode is not the same as native CPU instructions. Other implementations can use different strategies, including just-in-time compilation. A language name alone does not establish a universal execution mechanism.'
  ],diagram:'Source text → syntax/translation checks → executable representation → running operations\n                                              ↑ implementation-specific: bytecode or machine code'},
  {heading:'Read a small Python script without hidden prerequisites',paragraphs:[
   'The examples in this foundation course use Python as readable notation, but the reasoning applies more broadly. A line such as units = 2 + 3 gives the computed value five the name units. The equals sign means assignment. A quoted value such as "Started" is text. A function call uses parentheses after a name; print(...) is a supplied operation that displays a value. Later modules explain expressions, decisions and your own functions in detail.',
   'A script runs its ordinary top-level statements in sequence unless control flow changes that order. An interactive prompt may display a typed expression automatically; a saved script needs an explicit display operation such as print. A comment beginning with # explains intent to a reader and is not executed as a calculation. You do not need to install tools or submit code to complete this course: its knowledge checks are objective questions.'
  ]},
  {heading:'Classify failures before repairing them',paragraphs:[
   'A syntax error means the submitted text does not fit the language grammar. In an ordinary CPython module, source compilation happens before that module’s statements execute, so a syntax error later in that module can prevent an earlier print from running. A runtime error occurs when an executing operation fails, such as dividing by zero. A logic error can produce the wrong business result without producing an exception at all.',
   'These distinctions narrow a diagnosis. A missing parenthesis is not fixed by changing an input file. A correct-looking invoice total may still use an incorrect discount rule. Read the reported location and the surrounding operation, then compare actual behavior with the intended contract. Do not infer that a program is correct merely because it started or because no error message appeared.'
  ]}
 ],
 {problem:'A synthetic shipping script must announce its start and report the total of two pending batches.',steps:['Identify the literal text and arithmetic expression separately.','Trace the assignment to units before its display.','Predict the sequence of output lines before running the script.'],code:'print("Shipping job started")\nunits = 2 + 3\nprint(units)',expect:'Shipping job started\n5',explanation:'The first call prints literal text. Assignment evaluates the sum and binds units to five. The final call displays that value. This trace describes execution order; it does not mean the source text itself is a native CPU instruction stream.'},
 ['Treating compilation and interpretation as mutually exclusive labels hides the stages of real implementations.','Assuming a late syntax error must permit all earlier statements to run confuses translation with execution.','Equating “no exception” with “correct business result” overlooks logic errors.'],
 ['Separate source notation, implementation stages and runtime behavior.','A print call displays a value; assignment records a binding.','Classify syntax, runtime and logic failures before selecting a correction.'],['interpreter','execution','introduction'],[1]);

add('pf-machine-resources','m1.c2','CPU, working memory and persistent storage',
 'An inventory import does not use “the computer” as one undifferentiated resource. It calculates, holds temporary state and saves durable records through different mechanisms. Distinguishing those jobs helps explain why adding disk space may not fix a memory failure and why a successful calculation may still be lost after a restart.',
 [
  {heading:'Give each resource a concrete role',paragraphs:[
   'The CPU performs instructions such as arithmetic, comparisons and branching. Working memory holds active code and data used by a running process. Persistent storage holds files and other durable data that an application can load again later. A program can be limited by computation, memory capacity, storage capacity or the time spent moving data; these are different bottlenecks.',
   'This is a programmer’s model, not a claim that every machine has one simple physical layout. Operating systems provide virtual memory, caches and buffering, and some storage technologies have unusual persistence properties. Nevertheless, a name holding a value in a process is not by itself a saved database record. Durability needs a storage operation and a defined success condition.'
  ]},
  {heading:'Temporary state is not a saved snapshot',paragraphs:[
   'A process is a running instance of a program with its own execution state and resources. Restarting the process ordinarily begins a new execution; local variables from the old execution are not automatically restored. Assigning a value to another local name creates another binding, not an independent durable backup. Even a correctly computed result can disappear if it was never saved.',
   'A file write may also pass through buffers before a system’s durability policy is satisfied. Database transactions and durable-write guarantees address stronger requirements than simply changing an in-memory object. Do not promise that an operation survived every possible failure just because one API returned or one screen updated. Specify whether success means computed, accepted for saving, committed or durably acknowledged.'
  ],table:{headers:['Need','Resource or operation','What it does not prove'],rows:[['Calculate a reorder quantity','CPU instructions','That the result was saved'],['Hold active product records','Working memory','That a restart can restore them'],['Recover committed records later','Persistent storage and its durability policy','That every unsaved change survived']]}},
  {heading:'Connect resource limits to an import design',paragraphs:[
   'Loading an entire large export into a list can exhaust available process memory even when the file fits easily on disk. Reading records progressively can reduce peak memory. Repeating an expensive calculation many times can dominate CPU time; a suitable algorithm or avoiding repeated work may help. A nearly full storage volume needs a different response from a CPU-bound job.',
   'Measure before assuming the cause. An import waiting on a remote database is not necessarily using all CPU capacity. Swapping and caches can complicate observations. For this foundation lesson, the useful habit is to ask what data must be live at once, what work is being repeated, and which results have a real persistence contract.'
  ]}
 ],
 {problem:'A local stock calculation keeps a prior count under another name, then updates the current count. Decide whether either value is a saved record.',steps:['Bind the original count and a second name for its prior numeric value.','Rebind the current name after an adjustment.','Display both numbers and distinguish this in-memory trace from any durable write.'],code:'stock = 10\nprior_count = stock\nstock = stock - 3\nprint(stock, prior_count)',expect:'7 10',explanation:'The CPU performs subtraction and the running process retains the bindings. The second name still refers to the old integer value. No file or database write occurs, so neither binding constitutes a durable saved record. This example models state, not a backup system.'},
 ['Adding storage capacity does not by itself increase the memory available for a large active collection.','A second variable name is not a restart-safe backup.','Treating a displayed result as a durable commit can cause false success messages.'],
 ['Computation, active state and persistence have different contracts.','Resource bottlenecks need evidence rather than guesses.','State precisely what a success acknowledgement guarantees.'],['memory','execution'],[2]);

add('pf-representation','m1.c3','Bits, text encoding and representation limits',
 'A field can look like a simple number or character while its stored representation has important limits. Understanding bits, byte counts and numerical precision prevents corrupted identifiers, counters that wrap unexpectedly and incorrect assumptions about file sizes. You do not need electrical engineering to reason about these basic contracts.',
 [
  {heading:'Represent a whole number with place values',paragraphs:[
   'A bit has two possible values, conventionally written 0 and 1. In positional binary notation, places have weights 1, 2, 4, 8 and so on from right to left. The binary digits 1011 represent eight plus two plus one, which is eleven in decimal. A byte commonly contains eight bits. A pattern’s meaning depends on its agreed interpretation, not only on how the bits look.',
   'An unsigned eight-bit integer represents values from zero to 255. Under an unsigned modular arithmetic contract, adding one to 255 wraps to zero. Do not generalize that rule to every type or language: signed overflow rules can differ, and signed integer overflow in C is not a portable promise of wraparound. Python integers grow in precision subject to memory rather than silently wrapping at eight bits.'
  ]},
  {heading:'Distinguish mathematical values from approximations',paragraphs:[
   'A finite representation cannot store every possible real number exactly. Binary floating-point values approximate many familiar decimal fractions. An arithmetic expression can therefore be numerically close to an intended result without exact equality. Rounding a displayed string is not the same as changing the representation used in later calculations.',
   'Choose representations by the requirement. Whole-item counts need integers. Exact currency rules may use integer minor units or a decimal type with an explicit rounding policy. Scientific measurements often need floating-point arithmetic and tolerance-aware comparisons. A “more precise” display cannot repair an input that was already rounded incorrectly or a business rule that uses the wrong unit.'
  ]},
  {heading:'Text characters and encoded bytes are different measurements',paragraphs:[
   'Unicode assigns code points to characters; an encoding defines how code points become bytes. UTF-8 uses a variable number of bytes for different code points. Consequently, counting Python string code points is not the same as counting the bytes of its UTF-8 encoding. A visible symbol can also be composed from multiple code points, so user-visible length can require a more specialized rule.',
   'A file format or API must specify its encoding and which length limit it applies. If a database field allows a number of bytes, a character count alone may not establish that the value fits. If text is decoded with the wrong encoding, its displayed symbols can become incorrect even though bytes were transferred successfully. Preserve identifiers and messages through a defined encoding boundary rather than guessing from appearance.'
  ]}
 ],
 {problem:'An import needs to distinguish binary notation, a simulated eight-bit counter and the byte length of one accented code point.',steps:['Decode binary place values using a Python binary literal.','Simulate the explicitly unsigned eight-bit rule with modulo; do not claim Python itself overflowed.','Measure a string and its UTF-8 encoded bytes separately.'],code:'print(0b1011)\nprint((255 + 1) % 256)\nletter = "é"\nprint(len(letter), len(letter.encode("utf-8")))',expect:'11\n0\n1 2',explanation:'The binary literal represents eleven. Modulo 256 explicitly models an eight-bit unsigned wrap; Python first calculated the exact integer 256. The string contains one code point whose UTF-8 representation occupies two bytes. These are three different representation questions.'},
 ['Assuming every language and signed type wraps on overflow can produce nonportable or invalid reasoning.','Equating characters with bytes fails for variable-width encodings.','Adding decimal places to output does not restore numerical precision that was never represented.'],
 ['Bits need an interpretation: unsigned counts, signed values and text use different rules.','Representation limits depend on the type and implementation contract.','State both the encoding and the unit of a length or precision limit.'],['introduction','numbers','collections'],[2,3]);

add('pf-expressions','m2.c1','Evaluating expressions in the intended order',
 'A discount calculation can be syntactically valid yet answer the wrong question because its operations were grouped incorrectly. An expression combines values and operators to produce a value. This lesson shows how to evaluate one operation at a time, while keeping type conversion and display separate from the business calculation.',
 [
  {heading:'Evaluate by grouping and precedence',paragraphs:[
   'Parentheses explicitly group a subexpression. Multiplication and division normally bind more tightly than addition and subtraction. Therefore 5 + 2 * 3 is eleven, while (5 + 2) * 3 is twenty-one. Operators with the same precedence also have associativity rules; write parentheses when a formula’s intended grouping would otherwise be difficult to inspect.',
   'Do not infer the order from how an invoice looks on a printed page. Translate the requirement first: “apply a discount to the subtotal, then add shipping” is a different formula from “discount the subtotal plus shipping”. Give intermediate values names when that makes units or stages clearer. A longer expression is not necessarily a better implementation.'
  ]},
  {heading:'An operator’s meaning depends on types',paragraphs:[
   'The expression 2 + 3 is numeric addition. The expression "2" + "3" concatenates strings to make "23". Repeating text with multiplication, such as "A" * 3, is another defined string operation. Mixing a string and an integer with + does not automatically decide whether the caller meant addition or display text.',
   'Parse text explicitly before numeric arithmetic. A successful int conversion establishes an integer representation but does not establish a valid business range. Converting a fractional value to int can discard information; it is not a general rounding policy. If an input is required to be a whole count, reject a fractional string rather than silently truncating it.'
  ]},
  {heading:'Keep comparison and display out of the formula',paragraphs:[
   'A comparison such as total > limit produces a Boolean result. Assignment stores a binding; equality comparison with == asks whether values compare equal. The two operations are not interchangeable. In compound logical conditions, parentheses can make the intended relationship clear even when precedence would already produce the same result.',
   'Display formatting should express the result without changing the business rule. If the system stores money as whole minor units, keep the calculation in those units and format only at the output boundary. Define rounding when a division produces fractional units. A result looking reasonable in one sample does not prove its grouping, type conversions and boundary conditions are correct.'
  ]}
 ],
 {problem:'A small invoice has four items at 25 whole units each, a 10-unit discount on the goods and a 5-unit shipping fee.',steps:['Compute the goods subtotal using multiplication.','Subtract the goods discount before adding shipping.','Compare the final amount with the reporting threshold separately.'],code:'quantity = 4\nunit_price = 25\nsubtotal = quantity * unit_price\ntotal = (subtotal - 10) + 5\nprint(total)\nprint(total > 90)',expect:'95\nTrue',explanation:'The subtotal is 100, the discounted goods cost 90 and shipping raises the total to 95. The comparison produces True but does not alter the stored total. Every numeric value uses the same whole-unit convention in this hypothetical example.'},
 ['Dropping required parentheses can change which charges receive a discount.','Treating numeric-looking text as already numeric can concatenate instead of adding.','Mixing major and minor currency units produces a wrong formula even when every operator is valid.'],
 ['Trace grouping, operand types and units separately.','Explicit intermediate names help make formulas auditable.','Comparisons and display formatting are separate from the calculation they report.'],['introduction','control'],[2,3]);

add('pf-types-contracts','m2.c2','Choosing types that preserve the information',
 'A registration number, a seat count and a ticket price may all contain digits, but they do not have the same meaning. A type determines which operations are appropriate and which distinctions remain representable. Choose a type from the data contract before trying to force every field through a numeric conversion.',
 [
  {heading:'Ask what the value means, not how it looks',paragraphs:[
   'A whole count can use an integer; a measurement can use a suitable numeric representation; a yes/no state can use a Boolean. Text is appropriate for names, messages and many identifiers. An identifier such as "0042" is not necessarily a number for arithmetic: converting it to 42 loses its leading zeroes and may break matching with another system.',
   'A data contract should specify a field’s representation, allowed range, units and missing-value policy. “Numeric” alone is too vague for a price, timestamp or quantity. Avoid using an arbitrary string such as "unknown" in a field that otherwise contains numbers unless the surrounding schema explicitly handles that union of states.'
  ]},
  {heading:'Missing, false and zero are not interchangeable',paragraphs:[
   'Zero can be a valid measured count. False can be a legitimate answer to a yes/no question. Missing data means no value has been recorded. These states may require different decisions. For example, a student with no exam result should not be reported as having scored zero merely because the interface wants a number to display.',
   'In Python examples, None can represent absence while 0 represents a recorded numerical zero. A truthiness check combines several false-valued states, including zero, empty text and None. Use an explicit absence check when the distinction matters. Define serialization rules at an API or file boundary so a missing value is not silently replaced during transport.'
  ]},
  {heading:'Use a representation that fits the permitted operations',paragraphs:[
   'A date or time needs an agreed format and timezone policy, not just free-form text that happens to sort correctly in one sample. A currency amount needs a unit and rounding contract. A large identifier can exceed an external system’s exact numeric range even when Python can represent its integer value, so cross-system compatibility also matters.',
   'There is no universal “best type” independent of the task. Preserve the distinctions the business uses, reject values outside the contract and convert only at an explicit boundary. If two systems use different conventions, document the mapping and test representative values, including leading zeroes, empty inputs and permitted boundary values.'
  ]}
 ],
 {problem:'An LMS must display a learner reference exactly, while distinguishing an unassessed score from a recorded zero.',steps:['Keep the reference as text because its leading zeroes are meaningful.','Represent the missing score with None and the measured count with an integer.','Use an explicit absence comparison rather than a broad truthiness test.'],code:'reference = "0042"\nscore = None\nmissed_classes = 0\nprint(reference)\nprint(score is None)\nprint(missed_classes == 0)',expect:'0042\nTrue\nTrue',explanation:'The original identifier is preserved. The missing score and the real zero count satisfy different checks. A display can say “not assessed” for the first without inventing a zero result, while still accurately displaying no missed classes.'},
 ['Turning every digit string into a number can corrupt identifiers.','Replacing a missing score with zero invents evidence that does not exist.','Treating Boolean false and absence as the same state can overwrite a valid answer.'],
 ['Select a type by meaning and operations.','Preserve missing, false and zero as distinct states when required.','Specify units, ranges and cross-system representation at boundaries.'],['introduction','collections','execution'],[2,3]);

add('pf-state-tracing','m2.c3','Tracing assignment as state changes',
 'When a result is wrong, a state trace can be more useful than another guess at the final formula. Program state includes the values that names currently refer to. An assignment evaluates its right-hand side using the current state and then changes a binding. It is not a mathematical equation that continuously updates every related name.',
 [
  {heading:'Record the state after each statement',paragraphs:[
   'After x = 4, the name x refers to the integer four. After y = x + 1, y refers to five. Rebinding x later does not recalculate the earlier expression that produced y. The calculation occurred when that assignment executed. A trace table should therefore record a row after every relevant assignment, not just a list of expressions without timing.',
   'For x = x + 1, first read the old x, calculate a new value and then rebind x. This is a normal state update, not an impossible algebraic equality. In these examples, integers are immutable values; later Python collection lessons explain how shared mutable objects add another dimension to state tracing.'
  ],table:{headers:['Statement just executed','x','y'],rows:[['x = 4','4','not bound'],['y = x + 1','4','5'],['x = 9','9','5']]}},
  {heading:'Preserve a value before overwriting a needed binding',paragraphs:[
   'A common swap error is a = b followed by b = a. After the first assignment, the old value of a is no longer available through a. The second assignment copies the new a rather than the old value. A temporary name or a language’s multiple-assignment facility can preserve the intended pair of values.',
   'More generally, identify which old values a later step still needs. An import job may need both the original and adjusted quantity, and a report may need both a raw label and a normalized search key. Meaningful names such as original_count and adjusted_count make those roles visible. Retaining a prior value in memory still does not make it a durable audit record.'
  ]},
  {heading:'Test the transition, not only the final display',paragraphs:[
   'An intermediate error can cancel out in one example and still fail for another input. Compare the intended state after each meaningful operation. Start with small values you can calculate by hand, then include zero, equal inputs and distinct inputs. For an update such as increasing available stock after a return, write the expected old-to-new relationship explicitly.',
   'Avoid debugging by changing multiple unrelated statements at once. Form a hypothesis about the first incorrect transition, make a focused correction and rerun the relevant checks. A trace is an explanatory tool, not a substitute for representative tests, but it makes those tests much easier to interpret.'
  ]}
 ],
 {problem:'A warehouse report must preserve the original stock count while calculating the count after a return.',steps:['Bind the initial count and preserve it under a separate name.','Calculate the adjusted count using the current stock and returned quantity.','Verify that the preserved numeric value does not change when stock is rebound.'],code:'stock = 8\noriginal_count = stock\nreturned = 3\nstock = stock + returned\nprint(original_count, stock)\nreturned = 0\nprint(stock)',expect:'8 11\n11',explanation:'The original count remains eight. The stock update computes eleven at the time of assignment. Setting returned to zero afterward does not undo or recalculate that completed update.'},
 ['Reading = as an algebraic identity leads to false expectations of automatic recalculation.','Overwriting the only binding to an old value can make a later update impossible to compute correctly.','Checking only the final display can hide the first incorrect intermediate transition.'],
 ['Evaluate the right-hand side before rebinding the left-hand name.','Use a trace table to make timing explicit.','Preserve old values when the requirement still needs them.'],['introduction','execution'],[2]);

add('pf-conditions','m3.c1','Following decisions and repetitions in order',
 'An automated intake process might accept a valid request, reject an invalid count and repeat the procedure for several records. Conditions choose a branch; loops repeat a body. A careful trace records which branch was actually taken and which statements were skipped, rather than assuming every visible line runs.',
 [
  {heading:'Translate a decision into explicit branches',paragraphs:[
   'An if statement evaluates a condition. If it is true, its indented body runs. elif supplies another test when earlier branches were not selected, and else handles the remaining case. Exactly one branch of one if/elif/else chain runs. Several independent if statements are different: more than one of their bodies can run.',
   'Order overlapping tests from the intended priority. A high-score category must be tested before a lower threshold that would also match it if the categories are exclusive. Validate impossible inputs separately. A negative number of requested seats should not accidentally fall into a generic “small booking accepted” branch.'
  ]},
  {heading:'Trace a loop one iteration at a time',paragraphs:[
   'A for loop visits values supplied by an iterable, such as a list of counts. In a body, the loop variable refers to the current value. An accumulator such as total starts before the loop and is updated for selected records. A while loop instead repeats while its condition remains true; its termination depends on state changes or an explicit exit.',
   'A loop body may contain conditions, and a condition may contain a loop. Indentation shows that nesting in Python. Write down the current item, branch and accumulated state for each iteration. Include a case with no items, because the body may never execute and an initial value must still have a sensible meaning.'
  ]},
  {heading:'Distinguish skipping from finishing',paragraphs:[
   'continue moves to the next iteration without running the rest of the current body. break leaves the nearest enclosing loop. A return leaves a function, which is a different boundary explained in the next module. Put an update before or after a skip only when that placement matches the requirement.',
   'Do not remove items from the same list while relying on its forward positions unless you have designed for the shifting indexes. For a foundation-level filter, build a new result or update an accumulator without modifying the input sequence. This keeps the trace straightforward and reduces accidental omissions.'
  ]}
 ],
 {problem:'A synthetic registration batch contains positive seat counts and an invalid negative count. Total the valid counts and report the rejection.',steps:['Initialize a total before the batch loop.','Reject negative values and skip their contribution.','Add positive values and display the completed total after the loop.'],code:'total = 0\nfor count in [2, -1, 4]:\n    if count < 0:\n        print("Rejected")\n        continue\n    total = total + count\nprint(total)',expect:'Rejected\n6',explanation:'Two and four contribute to the total. The negative record prints a rejection and then skips the update. The last print is outside the loop, so it reports one completed total rather than a running total after every item.'},
 ['Treating separate if statements as an exclusive chain can execute multiple unintended branches.','Placing an accumulator update before a skip can include rejected data.','Assuming a loop body always runs overlooks empty inputs.'],
 ['Trace conditions and loop iterations using actual state.','Indentation determines which statements belong to a branch or body.','Skipping an item and exiting a loop have different effects.'],['control','introduction'],[5,6]);

add('pf-boundary-errors','m3.c2','Finding off-by-one errors and proving progress',
 'A small boundary mistake can skip the last shipment or process an extra record. A missing progress update can keep a job running forever. These failures are easier to prevent when the intended range and termination measure are written explicitly before the loop is implemented.',
 [
  {heading:'Specify the first value, last value and empty case',paragraphs:[
   'An inclusive business range and an exclusive programming endpoint are not the same notation. Python range(1, 5) supplies one through four. To include five with a positive unit step, the stop is six. For a collection with n positions, the valid indexes run from zero through n - 1, and there are no valid indexes when n is zero.',
   'Write the desired sequence for a small example. Then compare it with the actual range or condition. Tests with n = 0, n = 1 and a small larger n often reveal an off-by-one error more clearly than a large normal batch. Also consider whether a bound counts items or names the last included position.'
  ]},
  {heading:'Find a measure that moves toward termination',paragraphs:[
   'For a countdown loop, a nonnegative remaining count can decrease on every normal iteration. For traversal, an index can advance toward a finite upper bound. Identify what makes that progress happen and verify that every path which continues the loop performs the required update or exits.',
   'A continue placed before the only update can prevent progress on a particular input. A while condition that never changes can remain true forever. Do not merely add an arbitrary large iteration limit and call the underlying rule fixed; a safety bound can prevent resource exhaustion, but the intended termination argument still needs to be correct.'
  ]},
  {heading:'Separate safety, correctness and recovery',paragraphs:[
   'Production jobs may need cancellation, time limits and retry limits in addition to a correct loop. Those controls manage resource use and external failures. They do not establish that every intended record was processed exactly once. A retry policy also needs to avoid duplicating an operation whose first result was committed but whose acknowledgement was lost.',
   'At this level, focus on an explicit loop contract: which inputs are visited, what changes after one iteration and when execution stops. Record the expected output for boundary cases before running code. If a test fails, locate the first state transition that violates that contract rather than changing < to <= without checking the resulting sequence.'
  ]}
 ],
 {problem:'A report must list batch numbers one through four and then perform a three-step countdown.',steps:['Use an exclusive stop one beyond the required last batch number.','For the countdown, print the current positive value before decreasing it.','Verify the update occurs on every loop path in this example.'],code:'print(list(range(1, 5)))\nremaining = 3\nwhile remaining > 0:\n    print(remaining)\n    remaining = remaining - 1',expect:'[1, 2, 3, 4]\n3\n2\n1',explanation:'The range stop five is excluded. The countdown reaches zero through an update after each print, making the condition false. It neither prints zero nor relies on an accidental exception to stop.'},
 ['Using <= with a collection length can visit an invalid index equal to that length.','Putting continue before the only progress update can create an infinite loop for some inputs.','Changing a comparison without writing the desired sequence can replace one boundary defect with another.'],
 ['State endpoints and item counts precisely.','Check zero, one and small multi-item cases.','Every continuing path needs a termination argument or a deliberate bounded-wait policy.'],['control','execution'],[6]);

add('pf-loop-choice','m3.c3','Choosing traversal, repetition and early exit',
 'Different repetitive tasks have different stopping conditions. Processing every line in an export is not the same as searching until the first matching record or waiting until a changing condition is satisfied. A good loop makes its stopping rule visible instead of relying on a collection of unrelated flags.',
 [
  {heading:'Use traversal when the values are already available',paragraphs:[
   'A for loop fits a sequence or iterable whose supplied values define the work. Examples include visiting every line of a file, every item in a list or each number in a known range. The program does not need to manage a separate index unless the index itself has meaning. A readable loop variable should name the current item’s role.',
   'Decide whether all items must be visited. A total requires every included contribution, while a search for any matching item can finish after the first match. Both may begin with for, but their contracts differ. An early exit is appropriate only when later values cannot change the answer required by the caller.'
  ]},
  {heading:'Use condition-controlled repetition deliberately',paragraphs:[
   'A while loop fits repetition governed by a changing condition rather than simply exhausting a supplied sequence. Examples include a finite queue that is updated during processing or a bounded input-validation dialogue. The loop needs a progress, timeout or cancellation story; a condition that refers to an external system can remain unchanged indefinitely.',
   'Do not spin continuously while waiting for a remote service. A real polling design needs a delay, retry limit and a policy for failures. These concerns are outside a small local demonstration, but recognizing them prevents translating “repeat until ready” into an unbounded busy loop. Prefer the simplest control structure that expresses the actual work.'
  ]},
  {heading:'Exit at the boundary the requirement describes',paragraphs:[
   'break exits the nearest loop. It is useful for a search that has found the required answer. continue skips one iteration but does not finish the search. A helper function can return from a nested search when leaving one loop would not leave all the surrounding work. Choose the boundary explicitly rather than assuming one keyword stops the whole program.',
   'When the result is an existence answer, initialize a Boolean meaning “not found yet” and change it only on a match, or use an appropriate language/library helper after learning its contract. When collecting all matches, do not break at the first one. The correct loop structure follows the required result shape, not a universal preference for shorter code.'
  ]}
 ],
 {problem:'A local appointment list needs only the first available slot, not every available slot.',steps:['Traverse the supplied slot states in their existing order.','When an available slot is found, record its index and exit that search loop.','Leave the result as None when no slot matches.'],code:'slots = [False, False, True, True]\nfirst_available = None\nfor index in range(len(slots)):\n    if slots[index]:\n        first_available = index\n        break\nprint(first_available)',expect:'2',explanation:'Indexes zero and one do not match. Index two is the first match, so break ends the search before index three. Initializing to None distinguishes “no slot found” from a legitimate first position of zero.'},
 ['Breaking at the first match is wrong when the caller needs all matches or a complete total.','Using continue when the task is already complete wastes work and can change a later result unexpectedly.','An unbounded busy while loop is not a safe substitute for a polling and timeout policy.'],
 ['Choose a loop from its work source and stopping rule.','Match early exit to the required answer shape.','Distinguish no result from a valid zero index.'],['control','collections'],[6]);

add('pf-function-traces','m4.c1','Tracing a function’s inputs and returned result',
 'A function makes a smaller operation explicit: it accepts inputs, performs work and optionally returns a result. Following a call requires two related traces, one for the caller and one for the function body. This lesson explains the handoff without assuming that displaying a result and returning a value are the same operation.',
 [
  {heading:'Bind arguments when a function is called',paragraphs:[
   'A definition such as def add_fee(amount, fee): creates a function with two named parameters. A call supplies argument values. With positional arguments, their order determines which parameter receives which value; keyword arguments can identify parameters by name. The function body is not executed merely because its definition was encountered.',
   'Draw the call with concrete values substituted for its parameters. Then follow the body’s statements in order. Local parameter names belong to that call’s execution. They do not automatically rename the caller’s variables or update an unrelated global record. More advanced language-specific rules for shared mutable objects are taught separately in Python Fundamentals.'
  ]},
  {heading:'Return a value to the caller',paragraphs:[
   'A return statement ends the current function call and supplies a value to its caller. The caller can bind that result, compare it or pass it into another calculation. print produces visible output but does not automatically supply a numerical return value. A Python function that reaches its end without an explicit return value returns None.',
   'Statements after an executed return in the same function path do not run. Different branches can return different results, but the interface should keep their meanings clear. A helper that sometimes returns a count and sometimes prints an error while implicitly returning None forces every caller to guess what happened. Define a consistent success and failure contract.'
  ]},
  {heading:'Separate a reusable calculation from presentation',paragraphs:[
   'A function that computes a total without reading interactive input or printing can be reused in a report, a web request or a unit test. Its caller handles display according to that context. This separation makes a small calculation easier to verify and avoids changing business logic merely to change the wording on a screen.',
   'That does not mean all functions must be side-effect-free. Saving a record is an intentional side effect, but its contract should say so. Give effects clear names and return a meaningful outcome. Do not hide an email send or a database change inside a helper whose name suggests it merely calculates a number.'
  ]}
 ],
 {problem:'A hypothetical courier charge adds a fixed fee to a package subtotal, and the caller needs the returned amount for a report.',steps:['Substitute the call’s arguments into amount and fee.','Evaluate the sum inside the function and return it.','Bind the returned value in the caller before displaying it.'],code:'def add_fee(amount, fee):\n    return amount + fee\n\nsubtotal = 40\ncharge = add_fee(subtotal, 5)\nprint(charge)\nprint(subtotal)',expect:'45\n40',explanation:'The call returns 45, which the caller records as charge. The original subtotal binding remains 40. This is a returned calculation, not a hidden modification of the caller’s numeric input.'},
 ['Expecting print to substitute for return leaves a caller without the needed calculation result.','Assuming a function body runs during definition misorders the trace.','Treating a calculation helper as harmless while hiding persistence inside it makes the interface misleading.'],
 ['Trace argument binding, body execution and the return separately.','Return values can participate in later calculations.','Keep calculation, presentation and intentional side effects distinguishable.'],['functions','execution'],[10]);

add('pf-decomposition','m4.c2','Decomposing a business rule into testable operations',
 'A booking request can involve validation, pricing, storage and a confirmation message. Putting all of that into one long function makes failures harder to diagnose and rules harder to reuse. Decomposition means separating meaningful responsibilities while preserving the order and consistency requirements between them.',
 [
  {heading:'Start with responsibilities and contracts',paragraphs:[
   'List what the workflow must establish: whether input is acceptable, what a calculation means, which state change is needed and what can be reported afterward. Give each operation a clear input and output. Validation should not secretly save a record. A pricing helper should not depend on arbitrary screen text. Names should describe responsibilities rather than the physical location of code in a file.',
   'Avoid splitting a workflow into one function per line without a reason. A useful boundary has a coherent purpose and can be understood through its contract. Conversely, a function that parses input, chooses a discount, writes a database row and sends email often has several reasons to change. Separate those concerns so each can be tested without requiring every external system.'
  ]},
  {heading:'Make data flow and error handling explicit',paragraphs:[
   'A calculation should receive the values it needs rather than silently reading unrelated global variables. Its result should be passed into the next operation explicitly. This makes a trace resemble a pipeline: validated data, calculated decision, committed effect, reported outcome. The exact interfaces depend on the system, but hidden dependencies make every version harder to reason about.',
   'External effects introduce failure boundaries. If saving fails, the workflow must not tell the learner their enrollment is durable. If a notification fails after a successful commit, repeating the entire enrollment blindly may create a duplicate. A production design may use idempotent operations or a separate reliable notification mechanism. Decomposition exposes these concerns; it does not automatically solve them.'
  ],diagram:'Input → validate → calculate → commit intended change → acknowledge result\n                       pure work        explicit effect       truthful message'},
  {heading:'Test small contracts and their integration',paragraphs:[
   'A small deterministic function can be checked with representative inputs and expected outputs. Boundary cases matter: zero quantities, the smallest permitted count and a discount limit can reveal mistakes a typical example misses. Also test the integration order, because individually correct functions can still be composed incorrectly.',
   'Write down which assumptions each function requires and which facts it guarantees. A caller should satisfy those requirements rather than hoping a downstream operation will fix invalid data. Do not duplicate a business rule in several helpers with slightly different thresholds. A single authoritative rule with explicit callers is easier to maintain and audit.'
  ]}
 ],
 {problem:'A small reporting pipeline validates a whole-unit quantity, computes goods cost and applies shipping in separate deterministic helpers. It must not imply any purchase was saved.',steps:['Keep a validation predicate separate from arithmetic.','Compute goods cost once, then pass that result to the shipping calculation.','Display the result while explicitly leaving persistence outside this example.'],code:'def valid_quantity(n):\n    return n > 0\n\ndef goods_total(quantity, price):\n    return quantity * price\n\ndef with_shipping(goods, shipping):\n    return goods + shipping\n\nquantity = 3\nif valid_quantity(quantity):\n    goods = goods_total(quantity, 20)\n    print(with_shipping(goods, 5))',expect:'65',explanation:'Validation permits the positive quantity. The goods helper returns 60, and the shipping helper returns 65. These functions perform no persistence or notification. A production purchase workflow would add those effects through separately specified operations.'},
 ['Duplicating the same discount rule in several functions can produce inconsistent thresholds.','Hiding globals or side effects inside a calculation makes tests and callers unreliable.','Announcing success before the required durable effect occurs is a workflow defect, not a wording detail.'],
 ['Split by coherent responsibility rather than arbitrary line count.','Pass data and outcomes explicitly between operations.','Test both small calculations and their composition/order.'],['functions','execution'],[10]);

add('pf-algorithms','m4.c3','Writing and checking an algorithm before code',
 'An algorithm is a sufficiently precise procedure for solving a class of tasks. Pseudocode expresses that procedure without committing to every punctuation rule of a programming language. It is useful only when another reader can follow the same steps and reach the same result for the same input.',
 [
  {heading:'State inputs, outputs and rules first',paragraphs:[
   'Begin with what is supplied and what must be produced. “Find the earliest available appointment” needs an ordered set of slots and a definition of availability. It should also specify the no-match outcome. A procedure that omits that case is incomplete even if its normal example happens to work.',
   'Use unambiguous actions such as initialize a result, visit each item in order, compare a specific field and return a value. Words such as “handle appropriately” hide decisions rather than specify them. Pseudocode need not resemble Python exactly, but it must make branching, repetition and stopping conditions clear.'
  ]},
  {heading:'Trace the procedure with representative examples',paragraphs:[
   'A dry run follows the algorithm on a small input while recording its state. Include an empty input, a match at the first position, a later match and no match when those cases are permitted. These examples help reveal missing branches and off-by-one errors before language syntax distracts from the rule.',
   'A loop invariant describes a useful fact that remains true at a particular stage of each iteration. For a maximum search, the current best can mean “the largest item among those already visited”. Initialization, maintenance and the final condition must agree with that meaning. An invariant is not a magic proof: it must be appropriate to the contract and accompanied by termination reasoning.'
  ]},
  {heading:'Translate faithfully, then test the implementation',paragraphs:[
   'When turning pseudocode into code, preserve the defined order, comparisons and failure outcomes. Do not silently change “greater than” into “greater than or equal” when ties have a specified rule. If several implementations are possible, compare them against the same contract rather than assuming shorter code is automatically equivalent.',
   'Tests provide evidence for selected cases; they do not prove every possible input correct. Reasoning about the algorithm, reviewing assumptions and testing representative boundaries work together. For larger inputs, consider how the amount of work grows. A single-pass search can be simpler and less expensive than repeatedly rescanning an unchanged prefix.'
  ]}
 ],
 {problem:'Choose the largest positive capacity from a nonempty list of permitted capacities using one pass.',steps:['Initialize best from the first item so its meaning is established before comparisons.','For each remaining item, replace best only when a larger capacity is found.','Return the final best value after all candidates have been visited.'],code:'capacities = [12, 8, 15, 10]\nbest = capacities[0]\nfor capacity in capacities[1:]:\n    if capacity > best:\n        best = capacity\nprint(best)',expect:'15',explanation:'The best value is the maximum of the already visited prefix after every update. Twelve survives the comparison with eight, fifteen replaces twelve, and ten does not replace fifteen. The example requires a nonempty list; an empty-input policy must be added if the application permits empty input.'},
 ['Leaving the no-result or empty-input case unspecified creates an incomplete contract.','Using vague instructions in pseudocode hides decisions a programmer still has to invent.','Changing a comparison during translation can change the tie-breaking rule.'],
 ['Define input, output, exceptional cases and termination.','Dry runs and invariants make a procedure easier to inspect.','Translate the same contract faithfully and test representative boundaries.'],['control','functions','collections'],[5,6,10]);

